  "query_preview": "/MONDAY THROUGH SATURDAY/...\nIssued at 200 AM CDT Sun Aug 30 2026\n\nNext Week:\nAs we progress into Monday, the heat wave will continue to\nexpand across the region. 850mb temperatures push towards 21 to\n24 C, which is highly anomalously for late August. This will\ncorrelate to daily high temperatures in the middle to upper 90s,\nwith a few locations towards the Interstate 49 corridor pushing\n100. This pronounced heat wave will be characterized by\ntemperatures 10 to 15+ degrees above normal for late August, in\nwhich average highs are typically in the middle 80s. There is\nthe potential for some record temperatures as we progress\nthrough the week.\n\nThe well above normal temperatures and daily heat index values\nof 100 to 105+ degrees will be accompanied by minimal overnight\nrelief with lows in the middle to upper 70s. The extent of the \nextreme heat is well captured by the NWS HeatRisk product, with \nwidespread Major (3/4) to Extreme (4/4) Heat Risk over the area.\nThis outlines the potential for heat- related impacts, with \nincreasing concerns as the prolonged heat envelops the entire \narea. At this time, it appears the heat wave remains locked in \nplace through at least next Friday or Saturday. However, some \nguidance suggest the heat persists even beyond the seven day \nforecast period, with WPC and CPC highlighting extreme heat over\nthe central CONUS well into early/mid September. NBM \ninterquartile temperatures spreads increase (5 to 10 degrees)\nbeyond next weekend, though the signal is trending towards at \nleast the western half of the area remaining in extreme heat.\n\nCurrently, the highest heat index values of 100 to 105+ degrees\noverspread the area Tuesday through Friday. This time period is\nwhen dewpoints are progged to be highest, in the middle to upper\n60s. With increasing confidence, heat headlines will likely be \nneeded as we meet criteria of greater than 100 degree heat index\nvalues for 4 or more consecutive days. Check back for updates \nearly this week.\n\nThis period of extreme heat will be dry with no mentionable \nPoPs in the forecast through much of the upcoming week. The \nextent and coverage of this heat wave will further amplify \ndrought conditions over the area, with D1 and D2 now in effect \nfor portions of southeast KS and southwest MO. CPC continues to \nhighlight concerns for a rapid onset drought.",
